window.artFormDescriptions = {"Andaman_Nicobar":{"sculpture_art":{"region":"Andaman Nicobar (dataset folder association; precise cultural region requires review).","description":"Sculpture is a three-dimensional art category, not a painting style. Materials, subjects, and methods differ widely across Indian communities and historical periods. The folder’s images should be checked individually to identify medium, maker, provenance, and cultural context. This entry retains the dataset label without inferring a single regional school. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Andaman Nicobar → sculpture_art” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No specific history is asserted until the works and their provenance are identified.","characteristics":"To be documented after image-level identification.","themes":"To be documented after image-level identification.","materials_technique":"To be documented from the identified works and reliable craft documentation.","cultural_significance":"Requires community- and object-specific research before interpretation.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true},"Tribal_body_painting":{"region":"Andaman Nicobar (dataset folder association; precise cultural region requires review).","description":"Body painting is a broad practice found in different communities and ceremonial contexts. Designs, materials, and meanings are specific to the people and occasion involved, so a generic label cannot support claims about one tribe or symbolic code. The folder is retained as supplied; its images need community and source verification before a detailed cultural interpretation is published. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Andaman Nicobar → Tribal_body_painting” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No specific history is asserted until the works and their provenance are identified.","characteristics":"To be documented after image-level identification.","themes":"To be documented after image-level identification.","materials_technique":"To be documented from the identified works and reliable craft documentation.","cultural_significance":"Requires community- and object-specific research before interpretation.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true}},"Andhra_Pradesh":{"Kalamkari":{"region":"Andhra Pradesh, especially Srikalahasti and Machilipatnam; related painted and block-printed branches.","description":"Kalamkari is a painted and printed textile tradition, particularly associated with Andhra Pradesh. Its name refers to work made with a pen-like kalam, although block printing is also part of the craft. Srikalahasti work is known for drawn narrative compositions, while Machilipatnam is strongly associated with block-printed cloth; these are related but distinct practices. Cotton cloth, repeated dyeing, resist, and hand drawing create layered pattern and story. Motifs include religious narratives, plants, and ornament. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Andhra Pradesh → Kalamkari” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"The craft has regional workshop histories shaped by temple, court, and textile markets.","characteristics":"Detailed line drawing or block-printed pattern, layered color, and narrative or vegetal motifs.","themes":"Mythological stories, devotional figures, floral forms, and textile ornament.","materials_technique":"Cotton, mordants, resist, natural or modern dyes, hand-drawn pen work, and blocks; process differs by center.","cultural_significance":"Kalamkari joins painting and textile knowledge and sustains artisan communities in Andhra Pradesh.","sources":["https://lepakshihandicrafts.gov.in/our-heritage","https://handicrafts.nic.in/pdf/LIST_OF_CRAFT_REGISTRED_UNDER_GEOGRAPHICAL_INDICATION_HANDICRAFTS.pdf"],"manual_review":false},"Lepakshi":{"region":"Lepakshi, Andhra Pradesh.","description":"Lepakshi is best understood here as a reference to the historic temple complex and its mural and sculptural program, rather than a single portable painting genre. The temple’s painted ceilings and walls form part of a larger Vijayanagara-era architectural ensemble. Figures, ornament, and narrative scenes should be interpreted in relation to the building and its devotional setting. Because the folder name may also refer to the state handicrafts corporation, each image needs review before it is attributed to the temple itself. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Andhra Pradesh → Lepakshi” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"The Lepakshi temple complex is associated with Vijayanagara-period architecture and visual culture.","characteristics":"Architectural painting and sculpture integrated with temple space; individual scenes need site-level attribution.","themes":"Religious narrative, deities, and ornamental programs.","materials_technique":"Historic murals use wall-based painting methods; the exact condition and materials require object-specific conservation documentation.","cultural_significance":"The site preserves an important regional architectural and devotional heritage.","sources":["https://lepakshihandicrafts.gov.in/our-heritage","https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum"],"manual_review":true}},"Arunachal_Pradesh":{"Buddhist_Art":{"region":"Arunachal Pradesh (dataset folder association; precise cultural region requires review).","description":"This broad label can include paintings and devotional images made in Buddhist communities across the Himalayan region. The folder name does not identify one single school, workshop, or medium. Works may use iconographic conventions for Buddhas, teachers, mandalas, and narratives, but attribution requires object-level evidence. This overview gives cultural context while avoiding claims that a specific work originated in the state where it is filed. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Arunachal Pradesh → Buddhist_Art” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No specific history is asserted until the works and their provenance are identified.","characteristics":"To be documented after image-level identification.","themes":"To be documented after image-level identification.","materials_technique":"To be documented from the identified works and reliable craft documentation.","cultural_significance":"Requires community- and object-specific research before interpretation.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true},"Thangka":{"region":"Himalayan Buddhist communities, including Tibetan Buddhist regions of India; not exclusive to one state.","description":"Thangka is a portable painted or embroidered image used in Himalayan Buddhist religious life. A carefully measured figure or mandala is made on prepared cloth, then mounted with a textile border so it can be displayed, carried, or rolled. Deities, teachers, mandalas, and narrative scenes follow traditions of iconographic proportion. Styles vary across lineages and regions. The same art-form folder name appears in multiple states in this dataset; each page keeps its state folder while explaining the wider Himalayan context. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Arunachal Pradesh → Thangka” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"A longstanding devotional image tradition across Himalayan Buddhist cultures.","characteristics":"Portable scroll format, precise iconography, central figures, and textile mounting.","themes":"Buddhas, bodhisattvas, teachers, protective deities, mandalas, and Buddhist narratives.","materials_technique":"Prepared cloth, mineral and other pigments, fine brushes, and brocade mounting are common features.","cultural_significance":"Thangkas support teaching, meditation, ritual, and the transmission of religious knowledge.","sources":["https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://ccrtindia.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/Living-of-Tradition-Tribal-Painting.pdf"],"manual_review":false}},"Assam":{"Assamese_Manuscript":{"region":"Assam, particularly Vaishnavite sattra institutions and manuscript centers.","description":"Assamese manuscript painting refers to illustrated pages accompanying religious and literary manuscripts in Assam. Images and ornament were made within a manuscript culture that transmitted devotional texts, stories, and learning. Palm leaf and handmade paper supports are both found in regional manuscript traditions, and visual styles vary by period and institution. The dataset’s label is broad, so this page describes the medium and cultural setting without assigning every image to one workshop or date. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Assam → Assamese_Manuscript” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"Manuscript illustration grew within Assam’s literary and Vaishnavite religious institutions.","characteristics":"Compact illustrations integrated with text, clear contours, and ornamental page design.","themes":"Devotional stories, epic episodes, religious teaching, and literary subjects.","materials_technique":"Illustration on manuscript materials such as palm leaf or paper using ink and pigment; practices vary.","cultural_significance":"Painted manuscripts join visual art with the preservation and circulation of Assamese texts.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html","https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum"],"manual_review":true},"Traditional_Assamese_Art":{"region":"Assam (dataset folder association; precise cultural region requires review).","description":"This folder title does not point to one single, well-defined painting school. Assam has distinct manuscript, devotional, textile, and community art practices, but the images need object-level review before any one of those traditions can be assigned. The page therefore keeps the dataset label, describes the regional context cautiously, and avoids guessing at materials, dates, or community identity. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Assam → Traditional_Assamese_Art” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No specific history is asserted until the works and their provenance are identified.","characteristics":"To be documented after image-level identification.","themes":"To be documented after image-level identification.","materials_technique":"To be documented from the identified works and reliable craft documentation.","cultural_significance":"Requires community- and object-specific research before interpretation.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true}},"Bihar":{"Madhubani":{"region":"Mithila region of Bihar, with related practice across the border in Nepal’s Terai.","description":"Madhubani, also called Mithila painting, is a community-based painting tradition associated with the Mithila region. Women traditionally painted walls and floors for weddings, births, and festivals; artists later adapted the work to paper and cloth. Its compositions often fill the surface with fine outlines, repeated patterns, plants, animals, and narrative or devotional figures. Line-led kachni and filled-color bharni are commonly described approaches, alongside other local variants. The tradition continues to change as artists address contemporary subjects while keeping recognizable regional visual vocabularies. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Bihar → Madhubani” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"Traditionally painted on domestic walls and floors in Mithila; paper and cloth became important supports in the twentieth century.","characteristics":"Dense patterning, clear contours, limited empty ground, and locally varied line or color approaches.","themes":"Ritual occasions, deities, plants, birds, animals, and everyday or contemporary narratives.","materials_technique":"Historically earth-prepared walls, fingers, twigs, and plant-derived color; today paper, cloth, brushes, and commercial pigments are also used.","cultural_significance":"The practice carries household ritual, women’s artistic knowledge, and Mithila community storytelling into changing public and market settings.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Madhubani_Painting/Madhubani_Paintingwebpage.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/CmsUpload/20170506115215GI_BOOK_FINAL_ilovepdf_compressed.PDF"],"manual_review":false}},"Chandigarh":{"Modern_Indian_Art":{"region":"Chandigarh (dataset folder association; precise cultural region requires review).","description":"The folder is named “Modern_Indian_Art,” but this label alone is not enough to establish a single art tradition, medium, origin, or community. This entry preserves the dataset’s actual folder name and state/UT placement. It avoids unsupported attribution; the images require source and object-level review before the tradition can be described more specifically. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Chandigarh → Modern_Indian_Art” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No specific history is asserted until the works and their provenance are identified.","characteristics":"To be documented after image-level identification.","themes":"To be documented after image-level identification.","materials_technique":"To be documented from the identified works and reliable craft documentation.","cultural_significance":"Requires community- and object-specific research before interpretation.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true}},"Chhattisgarh":{"Godna":{"region":"Central and eastern India; the name refers to tattooing traditions and related painted adaptations in several communities.","description":"Godna painting adapts visual patterns associated with tattooing into painted work on surfaces such as paper, cloth, or walls. The term and practice vary among communities, and motifs can carry social, personal, or ritual associations that should not be generalized. Government craft references list Godna among India’s folk painting forms and associate it with multiple states. This record describes the broad practice while avoiding an unsupported claim of exclusive origin or one shared symbolic code. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Chhattisgarh → Godna” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"Godna-inspired painted work is documented across more than one state and community.","characteristics":"Repeated linear, geometric, and patterned motifs inspired by body-marking traditions.","themes":"Community identity and decorative pattern; meanings depend on local practice.","materials_technique":"Traditional tattooing uses skin-safe pigments and tools; painted adaptations use media appropriate to paper, cloth, or wall.","cultural_significance":"The practice translates embodied visual knowledge into other media; community-specific interpretation is essential.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/pdf/LIST_OF_CRAFT_REGISTRED_UNDER_GEOGRAPHICAL_INDICATION_HANDICRAFTS.pdf","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":false},"Gond":{"region":"Gond communities across central India; this folder is filed under the dataset’s Chhattisgarh state.","description":"Gond painting belongs to the wider visual traditions of Gond communities across central India. Contemporary painted works often build forms from fine lines, dots, dashes, and repeated marks, producing movement and texture across animals, plants, and everyday scenes. Artists’ individual styles are important, and there is no single fixed Gond formula. The art’s growing presence on paper and canvas reflects modern artist-led practice as well as community knowledge. Regional placement in this collection follows the folder, while the tradition itself spans state borders. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Chhattisgarh → Gond” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"A living community tradition with a contemporary paper-and-canvas practice; its history is not reducible to one state.","characteristics":"Layered line and dot patterns, animated forms, and detailed surfaces.","themes":"Animals, trees, community narratives, daily life, and the relationship between people and nature.","materials_technique":"Modern works commonly use paper or canvas with pen, brush, and pigments; practices vary by artist.","cultural_significance":"Gond artists carry community perspectives into contemporary visual art while maintaining distinctive mark-making.","sources":["https://indiaculture.gov.in/sites/default/files/annual-reports/AnnualReportMOCEnglish_2017-2018_25.05.2018.pdf","https://ignca.gov.in/the-bhils-of-madhya-pradesh/","https://ccrtindia.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/Living-of-Tradition-Tribal-Painting.pdf"],"manual_review":false},"Muria":{"region":"Chhattisgarh (dataset folder association; precise cultural region requires review).","description":"Muria identifies a community within the wider Gond-related peoples of central India, but the folder title alone does not establish a named painting school. The collected images may represent visual practice, craft, or community life. A source and object review is needed before assigning specific motifs, materials, or ritual meanings. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Chhattisgarh → Muria” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No specific history is asserted until the works and their provenance are identified.","characteristics":"To be documented after image-level identification.","themes":"To be documented after image-level identification.","materials_technique":"To be documented from the identified works and reliable craft documentation.","cultural_significance":"Requires community- and object-specific research before interpretation.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true}},"Dadra_Nagar_Haveli_Daman_Diu":{"Warli":{"region":"Warli community areas of Maharashtra, with related practice in neighboring Gujarat and the former Dadra and Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu region.","description":"Warli painting is a community art tradition associated with Warli Adivasi communities in western India. Its best-known visual language uses simple geometric human and animal forms arranged into scenes of work, celebration, and relation to the natural world. Traditionally, white rice-based pigment was painted on an earth-colored wall surface with a simple brush. Today artists also work on paper, canvas, and other supports. The familiar geometry is one part of a living practice whose meanings depend on makers and local settings. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Dadra Nagar Haveli Daman Diu → Warli” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"The tradition is practiced by Warli communities; claims of prehistoric continuity are uncertain and are not presented as fact.","characteristics":"Minimal geometric figures, rhythmic repetition, and high contrast between light pigment and earthy ground.","themes":"Community life, farming, animals, trees, ritual, and human relationships with the environment.","materials_technique":"Traditionally rice paste on prepared mud walls using a simple twig brush; contemporary materials vary.","cultural_significance":"Warli images record community knowledge and have become widely recognized while remaining rooted in living practice.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Warli_Painting/Warli_Paintingwebpage.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/pdf/LIST_OF_CRAFT_REGISTRED_UNDER_GEOGRAPHICAL_INDICATION_HANDICRAFTS.pdf"],"manual_review":false}},"Delhi":{"Miniature":{"region":"Delhi (dataset folder association; precise cultural region requires review).","description":"Miniature painting is a broad term for small-scale, finely detailed works on paper or other supports. India’s many court and regional schools differ in line, color, subject, and patronage; the label alone cannot establish a single origin. Depending on the object, themes may include courtly life, religious narratives, poetry, portraits, or nature. The state folder is retained as the collection’s classification, not treated as proof of a unique local school. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Delhi → Miniature” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No specific history is asserted until the works and their provenance are identified.","characteristics":"To be documented after image-level identification.","themes":"To be documented after image-level identification.","materials_technique":"To be documented from the identified works and reliable craft documentation.","cultural_significance":"Requires community- and object-specific research before interpretation.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true},"Mughal":{"region":"Delhi (dataset folder association; precise cultural region requires review).","description":"Mughal painting developed in imperial and courtly workshops and later influenced artistic practice across South Asia. Many works combine careful observation, portraiture, narrative scenes, and detailed settings. Court manuscripts, albums, and single-page paintings vary by ruler, workshop, and period. Since the Mughal tradition spread and changed across regions, a state folder does not imply that the work was produced there. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Delhi → Mughal” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No specific history is asserted until the works and their provenance are identified.","characteristics":"To be documented after image-level identification.","themes":"To be documented after image-level identification.","materials_technique":"To be documented from the identified works and reliable craft documentation.","cultural_significance":"Requires community- and object-specific research before interpretation.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true},"Traditional_Art":{"region":"Delhi (dataset folder association; precise cultural region requires review).","description":"“Traditional art” is a broad catalog label rather than the name of one identifiable painting tradition. This entry preserves the dataset’s category but does not infer a specific origin, medium, community, or history from the folder name. The images should be reviewed individually and relabeled when their subjects, materials, or provenance can be established. Until then, the page offers only general context about the diversity of regional artistic practice. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Delhi → Traditional_Art” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No specific history is asserted until the works and their provenance are identified.","characteristics":"To be documented after image-level identification.","themes":"To be documented after image-level identification.","materials_technique":"To be documented from the identified works and reliable craft documentation.","cultural_significance":"Requires community- and object-specific research before interpretation.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true}},"Goa":{"chitari":{"region":"Goa (dataset folder association; precise cultural region requires review).","description":"“Chitari” is used for artisan and decorative painting practices in Goa, but the folder label alone cannot establish a specific object type or workshop tradition. The images should be checked against Goan craft and architectural sources before claims about surface, pigment, period, or community are made. This entry preserves the supplied category and marks it for review. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Goa → chitari” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No specific history is asserted until the works and their provenance are identified.","characteristics":"To be documented after image-level identification.","themes":"To be documented after image-level identification.","materials_technique":"To be documented from the identified works and reliable craft documentation.","cultural_significance":"Requires community- and object-specific research before interpretation.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true},"kaavi":{"region":"Goa and the Konkan coast.","description":"Kaavi art is a decorative mural technique associated with Goa and the wider Konkan region. Its characteristic red designs are created against a pale lime-plastered surface, with the contrast produced through layers of plaster and selective removal. Floral, geometric, and architectural motifs appear in homes and religious buildings. The technique is vulnerable to building alteration and weathering, and regional practice is not identical at every site. It is a wall-surface craft rather than easel painting. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Goa → kaavi” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"A regional architectural decoration found on older Goan and Konkan buildings.","characteristics":"Red-on-white or pale-ground motifs, often with rhythmic floral and geometric patterns.","themes":"Ornamental forms shaped for architectural surfaces.","materials_technique":"Layered lime plaster and red lateritic pigment are associated with the technique; conservation methods vary.","cultural_significance":"Kaavi decoration gives local buildings a distinctive visual identity and records regional building craft.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":false}},"Gujarat":{"Pithora":{"region":"Western India, especially Rathwa and Bhil communities in Gujarat and Madhya Pradesh.","description":"Pithora is a ceremonial wall painting associated especially with Rathwa and Bhil communities of western India. It is made in connection with vows and ritual observances, with a painted procession of horses and other figures occupying the wall. The image is not simply a decorative scene: its making involves ritual specialists and community participation. Materials and arrangements vary by location and occasion. The tradition belongs to multiple communities across state borders, so a state folder should not imply exclusive ownership. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Gujarat → Pithora” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"A living ritual mural tradition documented among Bhil and Rathwa communities.","characteristics":"Large wall compositions, rows of horses and figures, and vivid color.","themes":"Ceremonial processions, deities, animals, and community narratives.","materials_technique":"Pigments on prepared wall surfaces; exact procedures differ between communities and ritual contexts.","cultural_significance":"The mural is part of a collective ritual act and should be understood through the communities who commission and make it.","sources":["https://ignca.gov.in/the-bhils-of-madhya-pradesh/","https://ccrtindia.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/Living-of-Tradition-Tribal-Painting.pdf","https://handicrafts.nic.in/pdf/LIST_OF_CRAFT_REGISTRED_UNDER_GEOGRAPHICAL_INDICATION_HANDICRAFTS.pdf"],"manual_review":false},"Rogan":{"region":"Kutch, Gujarat.","description":"Rogan is a specialized painted-textile craft associated with Kutch in Gujarat. A thick, oil-based colored paste is manipulated on cloth to form flowing, often symmetrical ornament; artists may draw a pattern without letting the tool touch the fabric. The resulting lines and motifs are distinctive, but the craft is practiced by a small number of specialist makers and processes can vary. The dataset label is a textile painting tradition rather than a generalized folk mural style. Contemporary production also includes new objects and adaptations. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Gujarat → Rogan” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"A place-based Kutch craft maintained by specialist artisan families.","characteristics":"Raised, flowing lines and balanced floral or geometric ornament on cloth.","themes":"Floral and abstract patterning, with designs adapted to contemporary products.","materials_technique":"Oil-based colored paste applied to cloth using a metal stylus or similar tool; details vary among makers.","cultural_significance":"Rogan preserves rare specialist knowledge and supports a distinctive Kutch artisan identity.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/pdf/LIST_OF_CRAFT_REGISTRED_UNDER_GEOGRAPHICAL_INDICATION_HANDICRAFTS.pdf"],"manual_review":false}},"Haryana":{"chowk purana":{"region":"Haryana; related domestic ritual floor and wall drawing practices occur across north India under different names.","description":"Chowk Purana is a domestic ritual drawing practice recorded in Haryana. Women make auspicious geometric or figurative designs on floors or walls for festivals, family ceremonies, and worship. The word chowk refers to a marked or enclosed ritual space, while local forms and names differ from village to village. These images are temporary and tied to an occasion, so a single fixed canon should not be assumed. The collection’s label is retained as found in the dataset. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Haryana → chowk purana” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"A household practice transmitted through local ritual and seasonal observance.","characteristics":"Geometric framing, symbolic figures, and locally varied motifs made for specific occasions.","themes":"Worship, festival cycles, family milestones, and auspicious protection.","materials_technique":"Traditionally drawn with locally available earth, lime, rice paste, or color on floor and wall surfaces; recipes vary.","cultural_significance":"The practice makes domestic space ceremonial and preserves women’s local knowledge.","sources":["https://ignca.gov.in/coilnet/bhit0001.htm","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":false},"Haryanvi_Folk_Art":{"region":"Haryana (dataset folder association; precise cultural region requires review).","description":"Haryana’s visual traditions include household ritual drawings, wall decoration, and other community practices whose names and methods vary locally. This broad folder label does not identify one specific style. Images should be reviewed for context before they are assigned to a named practice. This description keeps the state-level association while avoiding unsupported claims about a single canon or origin. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Haryana → Haryanvi_Folk_Art” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No specific history is asserted until the works and their provenance are identified.","characteristics":"To be documented after image-level identification.","themes":"To be documented after image-level identification.","materials_technique":"To be documented from the identified works and reliable craft documentation.","cultural_significance":"Requires community- and object-specific research before interpretation.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true},"mural":{"region":"Haryana (dataset folder association; precise cultural region requires review).","description":"A mural is a painting made for an architectural surface. This folder name does not identify a specific school, community, building, or period. Indian murals range from temple and palace programs to domestic and community wall painting, with different grounds, pigments, iconographies, and ritual roles. The images require review against their source and visual details before a more precise tradition can be described. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Haryana → mural” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No specific history is asserted until the works and their provenance are identified.","characteristics":"To be documented after image-level identification.","themes":"To be documented after image-level identification.","materials_technique":"To be documented from the identified works and reliable craft documentation.","cultural_significance":"Requires community- and object-specific research before interpretation.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true}},"Himachal_Pradesh":{"Chamba":{"region":"Chamba, Himachal Pradesh, within the wider Pahari cultural region.","description":"Chamba is a hill-region painting tradition associated with Himachal Pradesh. The folder name can refer to more than one Chamba art practice, including miniature painting and embroidered rumal work, so the medium should be confirmed from the images. Chamba miniatures belong to the broader Pahari painting world, while Chamba rumals are embroidered textiles with their own techniques. The page retains the folder label and flags the distinction to prevent one medium from being mistaken for the other. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Himachal Pradesh → Chamba” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"Chamba’s artistic heritage includes court painting and embroidered textiles developed in the hill-state context.","characteristics":"The specific visual features depend on whether the image is a painting or embroidered textile.","themes":"Devotional and courtly subjects occur in regional works; confirm the medium and object.","materials_technique":"Painted works use pigment on paper; rumals use embroidery on cloth. Image-level identification is needed.","cultural_significance":"The region’s visual heritage reflects connected but distinct painting and textile practices.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Chamba_Painting/ChambaPaintingWebPage.html","https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum","https://handicrafts.nic.in/pdf/LIST_OF_CRAFT_REGISTRED_UNDER_GEOGRAPHICAL_INDICATION_HANDICRAFTS.pdf"],"manual_review":true},"Pahari":{"region":"Himalayan hill states, including present-day Himachal Pradesh and Jammu region.","description":"Pahari painting is an umbrella term for several regional miniature schools that developed in Himalayan hill courts. Styles such as Basohli, Guler, Kangra, and others differ in line, palette, figure treatment, and patronage. Many works illustrate devotional poetry, epics, portraits, and courtly life. A folder labelled simply “Pahari” cannot identify a particular school or workshop, so this page explains the larger family and flags the need for object-level attribution. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Himachal Pradesh → Pahari” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"Multiple hill-court schools flourished from the seventeenth century onward, with distinct local histories.","characteristics":"Fine brushwork and small-format painting; specific stylistic traits vary between schools.","themes":"Devotional literature, Krishna narratives, courtly life, portraits, and nature.","materials_technique":"Pigments on prepared paper are common in historic miniatures; materials depend on the object.","cultural_significance":"Pahari painting records regional court patronage and a rich exchange of literary and visual ideas.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/pdf/LIST_OF_CRAFT_REGISTRED_UNDER_GEOGRAPHICAL_INDICATION_HANDICRAFTS.pdf","https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum"],"manual_review":true}},"Jammu_Kashmir":{"Basohli":{"region":"Basohli in the Jammu division of Jammu and Kashmir, with related Pahari court painting networks.","description":"Basohli painting is an early Pahari court style associated with the hill town of Basohli. Surviving works are celebrated for saturated reds, yellows, and blues, strong contours, stylized faces, and emotionally charged gestures. Series illustrating poetry and devotional narratives show how court patronage and literary culture shaped the style. Basohli should be understood as a regional school within the wider Pahari painting world, not as a synonym for all Himalayan miniature painting. Later schools developed their own distinct visual language. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Jammu Kashmir → Basohli” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"The style flourished in the late seventeenth and early eighteenth centuries under regional court patronage.","characteristics":"Bold color fields, emphatic outlines, expressive eyes, and richly patterned clothing and settings.","themes":"Devotional poetry, Krishna narratives, epics, portraits, and courtly subjects.","materials_technique":"Opaque pigments and fine brushwork on prepared paper are characteristic of surviving miniatures; individual materials vary.","cultural_significance":"Basohli works preserve a distinctive courtly visual language and a major chapter in Pahari painting.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Basohli_Painting/BasohliPaintingWebPage.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/pdf/LIST_OF_CRAFT_REGISTRED_UNDER_GEOGRAPHICAL_INDICATION_HANDICRAFTS.pdf"],"manual_review":false},"Kashmiri_Art":{"region":"Kashmir Valley and adjoining areas; the folder name is broader than one painting tradition.","description":"Kashmiri art is a broad regional label covering many media and periods, including manuscript, decorative, and contemporary practices. The folder does not name a particular school or medium, so no single history or motif set can be assigned safely. The collection’s state/UT placement is preserved, while the images need provenance and object-level identification for a more specific description. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Jammu Kashmir → Kashmiri_Art” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"The Kashmir region has varied artistic histories; this broad label does not define one chronology.","characteristics":"Undetermined from the folder name alone; review individual works.","themes":"Require identification of the pictured medium and source.","materials_technique":"Require object-level examination and reliable provenance.","cultural_significance":"Kashmir’s artistic traditions are diverse and should be represented with medium- and community-specific context.","sources":["https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true},"Pahari":{"region":"Himalayan hill states, including present-day Himachal Pradesh and Jammu region.","description":"Pahari painting is an umbrella term for several regional miniature schools that developed in Himalayan hill courts. Styles such as Basohli, Guler, Kangra, and others differ in line, palette, figure treatment, and patronage. Many works illustrate devotional poetry, epics, portraits, and courtly life. A folder labelled simply “Pahari” cannot identify a particular school or workshop, so this page explains the larger family and flags the need for object-level attribution. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Jammu Kashmir → Pahari” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"Multiple hill-court schools flourished from the seventeenth century onward, with distinct local histories.","characteristics":"Fine brushwork and small-format painting; specific stylistic traits vary between schools.","themes":"Devotional literature, Krishna narratives, courtly life, portraits, and nature.","materials_technique":"Pigments on prepared paper are common in historic miniatures; materials depend on the object.","cultural_significance":"Pahari painting records regional court patronage and a rich exchange of literary and visual ideas.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/pdf/LIST_OF_CRAFT_REGISTRED_UNDER_GEOGRAPHICAL_INDICATION_HANDICRAFTS.pdf","https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum"],"manual_review":true}},"Jharkhand":{"Jadupatua":{"region":"Santal Pargana in Jharkhand and adjoining West Bengal.","description":"Jadupatua, also called Jadopatia in government craft listings, is a vertical scroll-painting and storytelling tradition associated with the Jadupatua community. Scrolls arrange episodes in panels and were carried by storytellers who narrated religious, moral, and social subjects from village to village. Some works were connected with rites for the dead. Bold outlines and expressive figures support oral performance. The tradition crosses the present state boundary, so the Jharkhand folder does not imply exclusive regional practice. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Jharkhand → Jadupatua” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"A scroll tradition practiced in Santal Pargana and neighboring parts of West Bengal.","characteristics":"Portable vertical scrolls with panelled narrative scenes, expressive figures, and strong color.","themes":"Religious stories, moral narratives, and life-cycle subjects.","materials_technique":"Paper or cloth scrolls, natural and commercial pigments, and hand-built brushes are documented.","cultural_significance":"The paintings connect itinerant storytelling with ritual and community memory.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Jadopatia_Painting/JadopatiaPaintingWebPage.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/pdf/LIST_OF_CRAFT_REGISTRED_UNDER_GEOGRAPHICAL_INDICATION_HANDICRAFTS.pdf"],"manual_review":false},"Khovar":{"region":"Hazaribagh district, Jharkhand.","description":"Khovar is a wall-painting practice of the Hazaribagh region, traditionally associated with wedding-season decoration. Contrasting layers of clay are applied to a wall and then incised or scraped to reveal designs. Animals, plants, and geometric patterns are common, with local variations among artists and villages. Khovar is related in the region to Sohrai but has its own timing and method; the names should not be used as synonyms. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Jharkhand → Khovar” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"A living Hazaribagh mural practice maintained by local artists, especially women.","characteristics":"Incised linework revealed through contrasting clay layers and repeated natural motifs.","themes":"Marriage, fertility, animals, and plant life, with meanings grounded in local practice.","materials_technique":"Clay wall layers, combs, fingers, or simple tools are used to reveal contrasting grounds.","cultural_significance":"The mural decorates domestic space for an important life-cycle event and carries local women’s knowledge.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Sohrai%E2%80%93Khovar_Painting/SohraiKhovarPaintingIWebPage.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/pdf/LIST_OF_CRAFT_REGISTRED_UNDER_GEOGRAPHICAL_INDICATION_HANDICRAFTS.pdf"],"manual_review":false},"Sohrai":{"region":"Hazaribagh district and neighboring areas of Jharkhand.","description":"Sohrai is a seasonal wall-painting practice associated with the harvest and cattle festival period in Jharkhand. Artists paint animal, plant, and household motifs on earthen walls, using local colors and tools. It is closely related geographically to Khovar, but the two are distinguished by occasion and technique in many accounts. Patterns vary between communities and villages. Sohrai is a living domestic art and should be interpreted in its local ceremonial setting rather than as a generic tribal style. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Jharkhand → Sohrai” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"A seasonal mural practice linked with harvest and cattle-related observances in Jharkhand.","characteristics":"Earth-toned wall painting with animals, plants, and flowing decorative forms.","themes":"Harvest, cattle, fertility, and the natural environment.","materials_technique":"Clay and mineral pigments applied with cloth, fingers, or simple tools on prepared walls.","cultural_significance":"The painting renews domestic space and expresses community ties to agriculture and animals.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Sohrai%E2%80%93Khovar_Painting/SohraiKhovarPaintingIWebPage.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/pdf/LIST_OF_CRAFT_REGISTRED_UNDER_GEOGRAPHICAL_INDICATION_HANDICRAFTS.pdf"],"manual_review":false}},"Karnataka":{"Mysore":{"region":"Mysuru and southern Karnataka.","description":"Mysore painting is a South Indian devotional painting school shaped by the artistic life of the Mysore court. Carefully drawn figures, balanced compositions, delicate ornament, and raised gesso work distinguish many examples. Gold foil may highlight jewelry, garments, and architectural details. Subjects often include Hindu deities and stories, alongside royal or portrait imagery. Although related historically to broader southern and Vijayanagara-era traditions, Mysore painting has its own workshop history and should not be conflated with Thanjavur painting. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Karnataka → Mysore” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"The school received major court support under the Wodeyars, especially in the nineteenth century.","characteristics":"Fine line, restrained composition, soft color, raised gesso, and gold accents.","themes":"Hindu devotional figures and narratives, royal subjects, and portraits.","materials_technique":"Prepared supports, gesso relief, pigments, and gold foil are described in government craft sources.","cultural_significance":"The style reflects Mysore court patronage and enduring devotional image-making in Karnataka.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Mysore_Painting/Mysore_Paintingwebpage.html","https://mysore.nic.in/en/mysuru-specialities/","https://www.pib.gov.in/newsite/PrintRelease.aspx?lang=2&reg=48&relid=95489"],"manual_review":false}},"Kerala":{"Kalamezhuthu":{"region":"Kerala, especially ritual settings in temples and sacred groves.","description":"Kalamezhuthu is a ritual floor-drawing practice of Kerala, rather than a portable painting school. Artists make large colored images of deities on a prepared floor using finely powdered natural materials. The image is temporary: ritual singing or performance accompanies it, and it is erased after the observance. Patterns, colors, and iconography relate to the deity and local ritual tradition. The folder’s placement under Kerala is appropriate, but practices and names vary between communities and shrines. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Kerala → Kalamezhuthu” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"A living ritual art associated with devotional observances in Kerala.","characteristics":"Large, carefully proportioned floor images made from colored powders and removed after ritual use.","themes":"Deities and ritual iconography specific to the shrine or observance.","materials_technique":"Powders from natural materials are placed by hand on the floor; color sets and procedures vary.","cultural_significance":"The making, performance, and erasure form part of the ritual itself.","sources":["https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html"],"manual_review":false},"Kerala_Mural":{"region":"Kerala, especially temple and palace mural sites.","description":"Kerala mural painting refers to wall paintings found in religious and courtly settings across Kerala. Large figures, clear outlines, patterned ornament, and a rich palette are among the features often noted in surviving works. Subjects include Hindu deities and narrative episodes, interpreted through regional conventions. The term covers works from different sites and periods, so it should not be treated as one uniform school. This overview describes a broad mural tradition; site-specific attribution requires looking at the individual painting and its context. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Kerala → Kerala_Mural” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"Surviving mural cycles belong to different historical buildings and patronage contexts across Kerala.","characteristics":"Monumental wall-scale figures, decorative detail, and regionally distinctive color and line.","themes":"Deities, mythological narratives, and religious subjects.","materials_technique":"Traditional wall grounds and mineral or plant color are described for historic examples; conservation and later practice vary.","cultural_significance":"The murals connect visual storytelling with temple, palace, and community histories.","sources":["https://ccrtindia.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/Living-of-Tradition-Tribal-Painting.pdf","https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html"],"manual_review":false}},"Ladakh":{"Buddhist_Art":{"region":"Ladakh (dataset folder association; precise cultural region requires review).","description":"This broad label can include paintings and devotional images made in Buddhist communities across the Himalayan region. The folder name does not identify one single school, workshop, or medium. Works may use iconographic conventions for Buddhas, teachers, mandalas, and narratives, but attribution requires object-level evidence. This overview gives cultural context while avoiding claims that a specific work originated in the state where it is filed. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Ladakh → Buddhist_Art” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No specific history is asserted until the works and their provenance are identified.","characteristics":"To be documented after image-level identification.","themes":"To be documented after image-level identification.","materials_technique":"To be documented from the identified works and reliable craft documentation.","cultural_significance":"Requires community- and object-specific research before interpretation.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true},"Thangka":{"region":"Himalayan Buddhist communities, including Tibetan Buddhist regions of India; not exclusive to one state.","description":"Thangka is a portable painted or embroidered image used in Himalayan Buddhist religious life. A carefully measured figure or mandala is made on prepared cloth, then mounted with a textile border so it can be displayed, carried, or rolled. Deities, teachers, mandalas, and narrative scenes follow traditions of iconographic proportion. Styles vary across lineages and regions. The same art-form folder name appears in multiple states in this dataset; each page keeps its state folder while explaining the wider Himalayan context. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Ladakh → Thangka” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"A longstanding devotional image tradition across Himalayan Buddhist cultures.","characteristics":"Portable scroll format, precise iconography, central figures, and textile mounting.","themes":"Buddhas, bodhisattvas, teachers, protective deities, mandalas, and Buddhist narratives.","materials_technique":"Prepared cloth, mineral and other pigments, fine brushes, and brocade mounting are common features.","cultural_significance":"Thangkas support teaching, meditation, ritual, and the transmission of religious knowledge.","sources":["https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://ccrtindia.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/Living-of-Tradition-Tribal-Painting.pdf"],"manual_review":false}},"Lakshadweep":{"shell craft":{"region":"Lakshadweep islands.","description":"Shell craft uses shells in decorative and functional objects. This folder’s Lakshadweep attribution follows the dataset, but island practices and permitted material sources require careful documentation. Individual images should be checked for maker, object type, and provenance before assigning customary meanings. This is a craft category, not a painting school. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Lakshadweep → shell craft” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"The folder records a material craft category; a more specific history requires local maker or institutional sources.","characteristics":"Object-specific forms made from shell; no single style is implied.","themes":"Decorative and functional forms, depending on maker and use.","materials_technique":"Shell and associated finishing materials; verify the specific object and sourcing.","cultural_significance":"Local attribution should foreground island makers and responsible material use.","sources":["https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true},"weaving":{"region":"Lakshadweep islands; exact textile or mat tradition needs image-level identification.","description":"Weaving is a broad technique rather than one named Lakshadweep art form. The folder name does not specify whether images show mats, cloth, or another woven object. Materials, patterns, and uses must be verified from the object and local sources before a precise cultural description is possible. This record preserves the folder name and flags it for review. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Lakshadweep → weaving” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No single chronology can be assigned to the broad folder label.","characteristics":"Interlacing fibers or yarns; the specific structure is not identifiable from the folder name.","themes":"Functional design and pattern vary by woven object.","materials_technique":"Identify fiber, loom or hand technique, and object type from the image and reliable local documentation.","cultural_significance":"Requires local maker and use context to interpret accurately.","sources":["https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true}},"Madhya_Pradesh":{"Bhil":{"region":"Bhil communities across western and central India; the dataset folder is under Madhya Pradesh.","description":"Bhil painting is a living visual practice associated with Bhil communities across western and central India. Contemporary artists often use dense fields of colored dots to build figures, animals, plants, and scenes from lived experience and oral knowledge. The practice has moved onto paper and canvas through artist-led work while retaining links to community settings and visual memory. Bhil art is diverse, and neither dotted patterning nor a single regional location describes every Bhil artist or community. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Madhya Pradesh → Bhil” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"Contemporary Bhil painting is documented among artists in Madhya Pradesh and neighboring regions.","characteristics":"Repeated colored marks, vivid palettes, and figures drawn from community life and the natural world.","themes":"Animals, trees, daily life, memory, and stories specific to artists and communities.","materials_technique":"Paper and canvas with contemporary pigments are common in documented artist practice; techniques vary.","cultural_significance":"The paintings offer individual and community perspectives and expand recognition of Bhil artists.","sources":["https://ignca.gov.in/the-bhils-of-madhya-pradesh/","https://ccrtindia.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/Living-of-Tradition-Tribal-Painting.pdf","https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=2043016&lang=2&reg=48"],"manual_review":false},"Mandana":{"region":"Rajasthan and parts of Madhya Pradesh.","description":"Mandana is a domestic floor and wall drawing tradition practiced in parts of Rajasthan and Madhya Pradesh. Designs are made for auspicious occasions and household spaces, often using pale pigment against an earth-colored ground. Geometric framing, animals, and plant forms occur in locally varied compositions. Mandana is related broadly to other domestic ritual drawing traditions but has its own regional names and practices. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Madhya Pradesh → Mandana” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"A living household art practiced in western and central India.","characteristics":"White or pale linework, geometric organization, and motifs adapted to floor or wall surfaces.","themes":"Auspiciousness, household worship, seasonal occasions, and local motifs.","materials_technique":"Traditionally earth and lime or rice-based pigments on prepared floors and walls; practice varies.","cultural_significance":"The practice makes domestic space ceremonial and transmits women’s visual knowledge.","sources":["https://ignca.gov.in/coilnet/bhit0001.htm","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":false}},"Maharashtra":{"Warli":{"region":"Warli community areas of Maharashtra, with related practice in neighboring Gujarat and the former Dadra and Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu region.","description":"Warli painting is a community art tradition associated with Warli Adivasi communities in western India. Its best-known visual language uses simple geometric human and animal forms arranged into scenes of work, celebration, and relation to the natural world. Traditionally, white rice-based pigment was painted on an earth-colored wall surface with a simple brush. Today artists also work on paper, canvas, and other supports. The familiar geometry is one part of a living practice whose meanings depend on makers and local settings. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Maharashtra → Warli” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"The tradition is practiced by Warli communities; claims of prehistoric continuity are uncertain and are not presented as fact.","characteristics":"Minimal geometric figures, rhythmic repetition, and high contrast between light pigment and earthy ground.","themes":"Community life, farming, animals, trees, ritual, and human relationships with the environment.","materials_technique":"Traditionally rice paste on prepared mud walls using a simple twig brush; contemporary materials vary.","cultural_significance":"Warli images record community knowledge and have become widely recognized while remaining rooted in living practice.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Warli_Painting/Warli_Paintingwebpage.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/pdf/LIST_OF_CRAFT_REGISTRED_UNDER_GEOGRAPHICAL_INDICATION_HANDICRAFTS.pdf"],"manual_review":false}},"Manipur":{"Meitei":{"region":"Manipur (dataset folder association; precise cultural region requires review).","description":"Meitei names the majority ethnic community of Manipur and its broad visual culture, not one singular painting style. The region has distinct manuscript, ritual, textile, and contemporary art practices. Without a more specific source for the images, this page avoids assigning one medium, period, or symbolism and marks the folder for review. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Manipur → Meitei” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No specific history is asserted until the works and their provenance are identified.","characteristics":"To be documented after image-level identification.","themes":"To be documented after image-level identification.","materials_technique":"To be documented from the identified works and reliable craft documentation.","cultural_significance":"Requires community- and object-specific research before interpretation.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true},"Puyas":{"region":"Manipur, within Meitei manuscript culture.","description":"Puyas are Meitei manuscripts that preserve historical, religious, and literary knowledge in Manipur. The word refers to texts rather than one standardized painting style. Some manuscripts may include visual elements, but a folder labelled Puyas should not be assumed to contain a unified painting tradition. Object-level review is needed to identify manuscript type, date, language, and any illustrations. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Manipur → Puyas” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"Manuscript traditions connect to Meitei literary and religious histories; specific puyas have distinct provenance.","characteristics":"Text-centered manuscript forms; illustration and format vary by manuscript.","themes":"Historical, religious, cosmological, and literary knowledge.","materials_technique":"Manuscript supports and scripts vary; the folder images need identification before medium claims.","cultural_significance":"Puyas are important textual heritage and should be described as manuscripts unless an illustrated art form is verified.","sources":["https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true}},"Meghalaya":{"Garo_Art":{"region":"Meghalaya (dataset folder association; precise cultural region requires review).","description":"Garo communities in Meghalaya have distinct textile, material, and ceremonial arts. The general label “Garo Art” does not establish a named painting tradition, so this description does not treat the folder as a single style. Images should be checked for medium and community context before a specific history or meaning is attached. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Meghalaya → Garo_Art” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No specific history is asserted until the works and their provenance are identified.","characteristics":"To be documented after image-level identification.","themes":"To be documented after image-level identification.","materials_technique":"To be documented from the identified works and reliable craft documentation.","cultural_significance":"Requires community- and object-specific research before interpretation.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true},"Khasi_Art":{"region":"Meghalaya (dataset folder association; precise cultural region requires review).","description":"Khasi communities in Meghalaya maintain varied visual and material traditions, including weaving, woodwork, and ceremonial objects. “Khasi Art” is too broad to identify a single painting style or shared symbolic system. This entry keeps the folder’s wording while requiring image-level review to determine the actual medium, region, and maker. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Meghalaya → Khasi_Art” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No specific history is asserted until the works and their provenance are identified.","characteristics":"To be documented after image-level identification.","themes":"To be documented after image-level identification.","materials_technique":"To be documented from the identified works and reliable craft documentation.","cultural_significance":"Requires community- and object-specific research before interpretation.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true},"wood carving":{"region":"Meghalaya; maker community and object type require review.","description":"Wood carving is a three-dimensional craft whose forms depend on the object, maker, and community. This Meghalaya folder label is broad and does not identify a single carving school. The images should be reviewed to determine wood type, function, and local attribution. It is presented as craft, not painting. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Meghalaya → wood carving” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No one chronology is implied by this broad folder label.","characteristics":"Carved forms vary by object, tool, and maker.","themes":"Functional, architectural, ceremonial, and decorative uses are possible; verify each object.","materials_technique":"Carved wood using hand tools; species and process require local verification.","cultural_significance":"Accurate interpretation depends on maker and community context.","sources":["https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true}},"Mizoram":{"bamboo craft":{"region":"Mizoram and the wider Northeast; exact object type requires review.","description":"Bamboo and cane are widely used in Mizo craft traditions for practical and decorative objects. The folder label describes a material-based craft, not a painting school. Forms and weaving methods depend on the object and maker; baskets, containers, and household items should not be assigned meanings without evidence. This entry retains the dataset category and distinguishes craft from painting. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Mizoram → bamboo craft” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"A living material craft linked to everyday life in Mizoram and neighboring regions.","characteristics":"Lightweight, interlaced or constructed forms; styles differ by object and community.","themes":"Practical household and community uses, with decorative pattern where appropriate.","materials_technique":"Bamboo and cane split, bent, and woven or assembled; exact process depends on the object.","cultural_significance":"The craft carries material knowledge and skilled making across generations.","sources":["https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true},"puanchei":{"region":"Mizoram (dataset folder association; precise cultural region requires review).","description":"Puanchei is a traditional Mizo women’s wrap skirt woven with characteristic bands and motifs and worn especially for festive occasions. It is textile art rather than painting. Designs, colors, and names are connected to Mizo identity and weaving knowledge, with variations between makers and contemporary production. The dataset folder is kept as supplied; this page explains the textile medium accurately. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Mizoram → puanchei” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No specific history is asserted until the works and their provenance are identified.","characteristics":"To be documented after image-level identification.","themes":"To be documented after image-level identification.","materials_technique":"To be documented from the identified works and reliable craft documentation.","cultural_significance":"Requires community- and object-specific research before interpretation.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true},"shawl":{"region":"Mizoram (dataset folder association; precise cultural region requires review).","description":"The folder label “shawl” is broad: Mizoram has several named woven cloths, each with its own pattern and use. Woven textiles, rather than painting, are the relevant art form here. The image set should be reviewed to identify the specific cloth type before attributing a named pattern, social meaning, or history. This page provides regional context without treating all Mizo shawls as interchangeable. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Mizoram → shawl” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No specific history is asserted until the works and their provenance are identified.","characteristics":"To be documented after image-level identification.","themes":"To be documented after image-level identification.","materials_technique":"To be documented from the identified works and reliable craft documentation.","cultural_significance":"Requires community- and object-specific research before interpretation.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true}},"Nagaland":{"Naga_Tribal_Art":{"region":"Naga communities across Nagaland and neighboring areas.","description":"Naga visual culture includes diverse woven textiles, wood carving, architecture, adornment, and contemporary art. “Naga Tribal Art” is a broad label, not one uniform style: communities have distinct names, patterns, and histories. The images should be reviewed to identify the medium and community before a particular motif or meaning is explained. This description keeps the broader regional context without collapsing Naga cultures into one category. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Nagaland → Naga_Tribal_Art” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"Naga communities have distinct artistic histories; no single timeline applies to the broad folder label.","characteristics":"Varied by medium and community; identify the work before describing style.","themes":"Community identity, daily life, ceremonial practice, and contemporary expression, depending on object.","materials_technique":"Textile, wood, fiber, and other media may be represented; image-level verification is required.","cultural_significance":"Respectful interpretation must name the specific Naga community and maker where known.","sources":["https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true}},"Odisha":{"Pattachitra":{"region":"Odisha, especially the Puri and Raghurajpur area; related cloth-painting traditions also exist elsewhere in India.","description":"Odisha Pattachitra is a carefully outlined narrative painting tradition associated with Jagannath worship and the coastal region around Puri. Artists known as chitrakar make images on prepared cloth and other supports. Strong contours, compact figures, ornamental borders, and saturated areas of color help organize stories drawn from Vaishnava devotion, epics, and local practice. Pattachitra is not a single unchanging formula: palm-leaf work and newer formats have their own processes. The paintings serve devotional and storytelling roles as well as contemporary display. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Odisha → Pattachitra” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"The tradition is closely connected to Puri’s Jagannath religious culture and the hereditary practice of chitrakar families.","characteristics":"Firm line, decorative borders, tightly arranged figures, and vivid, mostly flat color.","themes":"Jagannath and Krishna narratives, episodes from epics, and regional devotional stories.","materials_technique":"Traditional cloth supports are layered and smoothed with a chalk-and-tamarind-gum preparation; mineral and other pigments and fine brushes are used.","cultural_significance":"Images support devotional life and preserve specialist painter knowledge while also circulating as portable works.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Orissa_Pattachitra/Orissa_Pattachitrawebpage.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/pdf/LIST_OF_CRAFT_REGISTRED_UNDER_GEOGRAPHICAL_INDICATION_HANDICRAFTS.pdf"],"manual_review":false},"Saura":{"region":"Saura communities of southern Odisha, particularly districts including Rayagada, Gajapati, Ganjam, and Koraput.","description":"Saura wall painting, often called Idital or ikons in craft references, is associated with ritual life among Saura communities in Odisha. The compositions use linked human and animal figures, household and agricultural activity, celestial signs, and a central sacred presence. Their arrangement can read as a visual account of community relations and ritual rather than as isolated decoration. Contemporary artists may use paper or cloth, but the customary setting is connected to domestic walls and ceremonial occasions. The name and meanings can vary by community and source. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Odisha → Saura” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"A living community practice documented in southern Odisha; descriptions of its ritual use should be understood in the context of Saura communities rather than generalized to all tribal art.","characteristics":"Linear figures, repeated forms, patterned movement, and strongly organized compositions.","themes":"Ritual, family and community life, animals, landscape, and sacred figures.","materials_technique":"Traditional accounts describe earthen wall grounds, natural colors, and simple brushes or sticks; modern supports and pigments also occur.","cultural_significance":"The paintings express Saura visual knowledge and ceremonial practice; specific meanings belong to community contexts.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Painting_of_Lanjia_Saura%28IDITAL%29/PaintingofLanjiaSauraWebPage.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html"],"manual_review":false}},"Puducherry":{"kolam":{"region":"Tamil Nadu and neighboring parts of southern India; practiced in Puducherry as well.","description":"Kolam is a daily and festive threshold drawing practice made with rice flour or other powders. A pattern is laid out at the entrance to a home, often using dots and continuous looping lines. Designs range from simple repeated forms to elaborate festival compositions, and they are remade regularly rather than preserved as permanent pictures. Kolam traditions differ across regions and households. The dataset’s Puducherry folder records local practice, while the form has a broader southern Indian presence. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Puducherry → kolam” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"A living domestic practice with daily and seasonal forms across southern India.","characteristics":"Dot grids, looping lines, symmetry, and repeated geometric or floral structures.","themes":"Auspicious welcome, household devotion, seasonal celebration, and generosity.","materials_technique":"Rice flour, chalk powder, or other powders applied by hand to a swept threshold or courtyard.","cultural_significance":"Daily making links artistry, household routine, hospitality, and ritual life.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html","https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum"],"manual_review":false},"Tanjore":{"region":"Thanjavur (Tanjore) and the Kaveri delta of Tamil Nadu.","description":"Thanjavur, or Tanjore, painting is a devotional South Indian panel-painting tradition associated with the Thanjavur region. Its iconic central figures, saturated colors, raised gesso ornament, and reflective gold foil create a sculptural, jewel-like surface. Hindu deities and devotional episodes are common subjects, with compositions designed for focused viewing and worship. The tradition developed through changing regional and courtly settings; it has related but distinct histories from Mysore painting. Modern workshop practices and materials can differ from older examples. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Puducherry → Tanjore” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"The style took shape in the Thanjavur region through early modern court and temple patronage.","characteristics":"Frontal iconic figures, rich color, raised relief, ornamental framing, and gold leaf or foil.","themes":"Hindu deities, devotional narratives, and temple-centered worship.","materials_technique":"Panel supports, gesso relief, pigments, and metallic foil are prominent features.","cultural_significance":"These paintings connect domestic devotion, temple imagery, and specialist workshop skill.","sources":["https://www.nationalmuseumindia.gov.in/en/thanjavur-paintings","https://www.pib.gov.in/newsite/PrintRelease.aspx?lang=2&reg=48&relid=95489"],"manual_review":false}},"Punjab":{"Punjab_Folk_Art":{"region":"Punjab; many related forms also extend into adjoining states and communities.","description":"Punjab folk visual traditions include embroidery, painted objects, ritual decoration, and other regional arts. The folder label does not identify one painting tradition, so this page avoids assuming a particular medium or community. The images need review for subject and provenance. Where the material is textile or craft, the site should describe it as such rather than as painting. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Punjab → Punjab_Folk_Art” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"A broad regional category with multiple histories and media.","characteristics":"Cannot be summarized as one style without identifying the images.","themes":"Vary by community, medium, and function.","materials_technique":"Verify whether each work is painted, embroidered, printed, or made in another medium.","cultural_significance":"The diversity of Punjab’s folk arts should be represented without flattening distinct practices.","sources":["https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true}},"Rajasthan":{"Pichwai":{"region":"Nathdwara, Rajasthan, with devotional links to the Shrinathji temple and Pushtimarg tradition.","description":"Pichwai paintings are large, detailed cloth images traditionally hung behind the image of Shrinathji at Nathdwara. Their visual program changes with ritual calendars, seasons, and temple festivals. Krishna, cows, lotuses, peacocks, and landscape details are arranged in carefully balanced compositions, often with elaborate ornament. The paintings are devotional objects shaped by temple service and specialist workshops. Smaller versions now circulate outside the shrine setting, but the form’s ritual context remains central to understanding its imagery. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Rajasthan → Pichwai” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"The tradition developed around Shrinathji worship at Nathdwara from the early modern period.","characteristics":"Large cloth format, detailed ornament, seasonal compositions, and carefully patterned figures and foliage.","themes":"Krishna as Shrinathji, temple festivals, seasons, cows, lotuses, and devotional episodes.","materials_technique":"Prepared cloth, layered pigments, fine brushes, and sometimes metallic highlights; practices differ by workshop.","cultural_significance":"Pichwais form part of a living temple visual culture and its cycle of devotional service.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Nathdwara_Pichhwai_Painting/NathdwaraPichhwaiWebPage.html","https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum"],"manual_review":false},"Rajasthani_Miniature":{"region":"Rajasthan, across multiple regional court schools.","description":"Rajasthani miniature painting refers to several related but distinct court schools, including Mewar, Marwar, Bundi, Kota, Jaipur, and Kishangarh. They developed under different patrons and show varied approaches to color, landscape, figure, and narrative. Religious poetry, courtly life, portraits, and literary themes appear across the traditions. The folder label is broad; an individual painting requires attribution before it can be assigned to a particular school. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Rajasthan → Rajasthani_Miniature” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"Regional schools developed over centuries in Rajasthan’s courts and workshops.","characteristics":"Small-format painting with fine detail; palette and composition vary among schools.","themes":"Devotional narratives, courtly life, portraits, poetry, and nature.","materials_technique":"Pigments and fine brushwork on prepared paper are common in historic works.","cultural_significance":"These schools preserve regional histories of patronage, literature, and visual exchange.","sources":["https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum","https://handicrafts.nic.in/pdf/LIST_OF_CRAFT_REGISTRED_UNDER_GEOGRAPHICAL_INDICATION_HANDICRAFTS.pdf","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html"],"manual_review":false}},"Sikkim":{"Buddhist_Art":{"region":"Sikkim (dataset folder association; precise cultural region requires review).","description":"This broad label can include paintings and devotional images made in Buddhist communities across the Himalayan region. The folder name does not identify one single school, workshop, or medium. Works may use iconographic conventions for Buddhas, teachers, mandalas, and narratives, but attribution requires object-level evidence. This overview gives cultural context while avoiding claims that a specific work originated in the state where it is filed. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Sikkim → Buddhist_Art” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No specific history is asserted until the works and their provenance are identified.","characteristics":"To be documented after image-level identification.","themes":"To be documented after image-level identification.","materials_technique":"To be documented from the identified works and reliable craft documentation.","cultural_significance":"Requires community- and object-specific research before interpretation.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true},"Thangka":{"region":"Himalayan Buddhist communities, including Tibetan Buddhist regions of India; not exclusive to one state.","description":"Thangka is a portable painted or embroidered image used in Himalayan Buddhist religious life. A carefully measured figure or mandala is made on prepared cloth, then mounted with a textile border so it can be displayed, carried, or rolled. Deities, teachers, mandalas, and narrative scenes follow traditions of iconographic proportion. Styles vary across lineages and regions. The same art-form folder name appears in multiple states in this dataset; each page keeps its state folder while explaining the wider Himalayan context. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Sikkim → Thangka” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"A longstanding devotional image tradition across Himalayan Buddhist cultures.","characteristics":"Portable scroll format, precise iconography, central figures, and textile mounting.","themes":"Buddhas, bodhisattvas, teachers, protective deities, mandalas, and Buddhist narratives.","materials_technique":"Prepared cloth, mineral and other pigments, fine brushes, and brocade mounting are common features.","cultural_significance":"Thangkas support teaching, meditation, ritual, and the transmission of religious knowledge.","sources":["https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://ccrtindia.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/Living-of-Tradition-Tribal-Painting.pdf"],"manual_review":false}},"Tamil_Nadu":{"Chola_Mural":{"region":"Tamil Nadu; associated with Chola-period temple mural heritage.","description":"Chola mural refers to wall paintings associated with temples built or supported during the Chola period in Tamil Nadu. Surviving murals are integrated with architecture and religious space, and individual sites have distinct histories and conservation conditions. They should not be treated as a single portable painting genre. This overview covers the architectural tradition; any image’s date and site need specific attribution. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Tamil Nadu → Chola_Mural” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"Chola-period mural evidence survives in temple settings, with major examples studied through site-specific scholarship.","characteristics":"Wall-scale painting integrated with temple architecture; condition and style vary by site.","themes":"Shaiva and other religious imagery, deities, and temple narratives.","materials_technique":"Paint on prepared wall or plaster surfaces; exact layers require conservation study.","cultural_significance":"The murals preserve religious, artistic, and architectural histories of South India.","sources":["https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum","https://www.pib.gov.in/newsite/PrintRelease.aspx?lang=2&reg=48&relid=95489"],"manual_review":false},"Tanjore":{"region":"Thanjavur (Tanjore) and the Kaveri delta of Tamil Nadu.","description":"Thanjavur, or Tanjore, painting is a devotional South Indian panel-painting tradition associated with the Thanjavur region. Its iconic central figures, saturated colors, raised gesso ornament, and reflective gold foil create a sculptural, jewel-like surface. Hindu deities and devotional episodes are common subjects, with compositions designed for focused viewing and worship. The tradition developed through changing regional and courtly settings; it has related but distinct histories from Mysore painting. Modern workshop practices and materials can differ from older examples. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Tamil Nadu → Tanjore” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"The style took shape in the Thanjavur region through early modern court and temple patronage.","characteristics":"Frontal iconic figures, rich color, raised relief, ornamental framing, and gold leaf or foil.","themes":"Hindu deities, devotional narratives, and temple-centered worship.","materials_technique":"Panel supports, gesso relief, pigments, and metallic foil are prominent features.","cultural_significance":"These paintings connect domestic devotion, temple imagery, and specialist workshop skill.","sources":["https://www.nationalmuseumindia.gov.in/en/thanjavur-paintings","https://www.pib.gov.in/newsite/PrintRelease.aspx?lang=2&reg=48&relid=95489"],"manual_review":false}},"Telangana":{"Cheriyal":{"region":"Cheriyal and the wider Telangana region.","description":"Cheriyal painting is a Telangana narrative scroll tradition related to the region’s nakashi picture-making. Painted panels arrange brightly colored figures in a sequence that can accompany oral narration and local storytelling. Mythological episodes, community histories, occupations, and folklore appear in a legible, theatrical style, often against a red ground. Scrolls were used as visual aids by storytellers and performers; smaller contemporary works adapt the imagery to new formats. The tradition joins painting with spoken narrative, so its meaning is carried both by the image sequence and by the teller. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Telangana → Cheriyal” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"The craft is documented as a regional Telangana scroll tradition used in storytelling; claims of very ancient dates are not treated as settled here.","characteristics":"Sequential scenes, bold outlines, vivid colors, expressive figures, and commonly a red background.","themes":"Epics, local legends, folklore, community stories, and social life.","materials_technique":"Prepared cloth scrolls, layered ground, pigments, and fine brushes are described in craft documentation; current materials may vary.","cultural_significance":"Scrolls preserve a visual storytelling practice and local narrative repertoires.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Cheriyal_painting/Cheriyal_paintingwebpage.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/pdf/LIST_OF_CRAFT_REGISTRED_UNDER_GEOGRAPHICAL_INDICATION_HANDICRAFTS.pdf"],"manual_review":false},"Nirmal":{"region":"Nirmal town and surrounding area in present-day Telangana.","description":"Nirmal painting is a wood-based painting tradition associated with Nirmal in Telangana. The craft’s painted panels are known for smooth grounds, decorative borders, bright color, and gold-like accents. Subjects include figures, birds, landscapes, and scenes inspired by literary or religious narratives. The town’s painting and toy-making traditions overlap in local craft history, though each object type has its own process. Contemporary Nirmal works use modern materials alongside inherited design vocabularies, so claims about a single historic recipe should be treated with care. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Telangana → Nirmal” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"Government craft documentation connects the tradition to Nirmal and local Naqash artisan communities; dates in popular accounts vary.","characteristics":"Polished wood panels, stylized figures, decorative detail, and luminous color accents.","themes":"Mythological or literary scenes, birds, landscapes, and local decorative subjects.","materials_technique":"Wooden supports with prepared grounds and painted layers; materials vary across contemporary production.","cultural_significance":"Nirmal painting sustains a place-based artisan identity and a broader Telangana craft ecosystem.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Nirmal_Paintings/NirmalPaintingsWebPage.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/pdf/LIST_OF_CRAFT_REGISTRED_UNDER_GEOGRAPHICAL_INDICATION_HANDICRAFTS.pdf"],"manual_review":false}},"Tripura":{"bamboo and cane craft":{"region":"Tripura and the wider Northeast.","description":"Bamboo and cane crafts form part of everyday material culture in Tripura and across Northeast India. The folder label covers a family of techniques and objects rather than one painting tradition. Weaving and construction methods vary with function and community. The images should be reviewed to identify the specific object and technique before any more detailed attribution is made. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Tripura → bamboo and cane craft” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"A living regional craft tradition with object types and methods that vary by maker.","characteristics":"Interlaced, coiled, or assembled forms in locally available plant materials.","themes":"Primarily functional forms, sometimes with decorative pattern.","materials_technique":"Prepared bamboo and cane strips are woven, bent, or assembled; technique depends on the object.","cultural_significance":"Craft knowledge links everyday use, local materials, and skilled making.","sources":["https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true},"mural painting":{"region":"Tripura; exact site, community, and period need identification.","description":"The folder label “mural painting” does not specify a named Tripura school, site, community, or period. Murals may be religious, architectural, or contemporary, and their materials and histories depend on the building and maker. This page preserves the folder classification while flagging the need to identify the images and verify their source before assigning a regional tradition. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Tripura → mural painting” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No single chronology can be assigned without identifying the site and works.","characteristics":"Wall-scale painting; composition and style depend on the specific building and painter.","themes":"Require image and site identification.","materials_technique":"Wall preparation and pigments vary; conservation or source documentation is needed.","cultural_significance":"Mural interpretation depends on its architectural and community setting.","sources":["https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true},"rise and rignai textile":{"region":"Tripura, among Tripuri and related communities.","description":"Risa and Rignai are named Tripuri textiles, not painting styles. Risa is a versatile upper-body cloth, while Rignai is a women’s wraparound garment; designs and use are connected with Tripuri identity and weaving knowledge. The dataset folder spells the name “rise and rignai”; this description preserves that exact folder name while using the commonly documented textile names. Specific patterns should be attributed only with maker or community context. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Tripura → rise and rignai textile” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"Living handwoven textile traditions; names and use vary among communities.","characteristics":"Woven garments with regionally recognizable patterns and color bands.","themes":"Clothing, identity, life-cycle use, and contemporary textile design.","materials_technique":"Loom-woven cloth; fibers and pattern structures vary by maker and garment.","cultural_significance":"The textiles carry weaving knowledge and community identity across generations.","sources":["https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true}},"Uttar_Pradesh":{"Miniature":{"region":"Uttar Pradesh (dataset folder association; precise cultural region requires review).","description":"Miniature painting is a broad term for small-scale, finely detailed works on paper or other supports. India’s many court and regional schools differ in line, color, subject, and patronage; the label alone cannot establish a single origin. Depending on the object, themes may include courtly life, religious narratives, poetry, portraits, or nature. The state folder is retained as the collection’s classification, not treated as proof of a unique local school. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Uttar Pradesh → Miniature” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No specific history is asserted until the works and their provenance are identified.","characteristics":"To be documented after image-level identification.","themes":"To be documented after image-level identification.","materials_technique":"To be documented from the identified works and reliable craft documentation.","cultural_significance":"Requires community- and object-specific research before interpretation.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true},"Mughal":{"region":"Uttar Pradesh (dataset folder association; precise cultural region requires review).","description":"Mughal painting developed in imperial and courtly workshops and later influenced artistic practice across South Asia. Many works combine careful observation, portraiture, narrative scenes, and detailed settings. Court manuscripts, albums, and single-page paintings vary by ruler, workshop, and period. Since the Mughal tradition spread and changed across regions, a state folder does not imply that the work was produced there. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Uttar Pradesh → Mughal” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"No specific history is asserted until the works and their provenance are identified.","characteristics":"To be documented after image-level identification.","themes":"To be documented after image-level identification.","materials_technique":"To be documented from the identified works and reliable craft documentation.","cultural_significance":"Requires community- and object-specific research before interpretation.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Landing_Page.html"],"manual_review":true}},"Uttarakhand":{"Garhwal":{"region":"Garhwal Himalaya, Uttarakhand; related Pahari painting schools developed across adjoining hill courts.","description":"Garhwal painting is a regional Himalayan painting tradition associated with the Garhwal area. The label can cover courtly and devotional works rather than one tightly standardized style. Paintings from the broader Pahari world often use fine line, layered landscape, and narrative figures, but individual works need provenance before a specific workshop or date can be assigned. This entry therefore treats Garhwal as a regional school and keeps claims about precise origin, chronology, or materials conservative. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “Uttarakhand → Garhwal” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"Garhwal’s painting history belongs to the wider network of Himalayan hill courts and devotional patronage.","characteristics":"Fine drawing and narrative compositions; features vary by date and workshop.","themes":"Devotional subjects, literary narratives, portraits, and courtly life.","materials_technique":"Historic works commonly use pigment on paper; attribution and materials are object-specific.","cultural_significance":"The tradition records regional patronage and artistic exchange in the Himalayan foothills.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html","https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum","https://ccrtindia.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/Living-of-Tradition-Tribal-Painting.pdf"],"manual_review":false}},"West_Bengal":{"Kalighat":{"region":"Kolkata, West Bengal.","description":"Kalighat painting developed around the Kalighat temple area of nineteenth-century Kolkata. Made by patua painters on paper, these works often use sweeping brush lines, simplified forms, and areas of bright color. Religious figures appeared alongside scenes of urban life and social satire, making the tradition responsive to a changing city. Kalighat painting is distinct from the itinerant Bengal scroll tradition, though both are associated with patua communities and storytelling. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “West Bengal → Kalighat” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"The style grew in the nineteenth century around Kolkata’s temple and market economy.","characteristics":"Bold flowing contour, simplified modeling, brisk brushwork, and uncluttered grounds.","themes":"Hindu deities, mythological scenes, urban types, and social commentary.","materials_technique":"Paper with opaque watercolor or other pigments and broad, controlled brushwork.","cultural_significance":"The paintings document popular religion and changing urban society through an accessible market art.","sources":["https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html"],"manual_review":false},"Pattachitra":{"region":"Odisha, especially the Puri and Raghurajpur area; related cloth-painting traditions also exist elsewhere in India.","description":"Odisha Pattachitra is a carefully outlined narrative painting tradition associated with Jagannath worship and the coastal region around Puri. Artists known as chitrakar make images on prepared cloth and other supports. Strong contours, compact figures, ornamental borders, and saturated areas of color help organize stories drawn from Vaishnava devotion, epics, and local practice. Pattachitra is not a single unchanging formula: palm-leaf work and newer formats have their own processes. The paintings serve devotional and storytelling roles as well as contemporary display. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “West Bengal → Pattachitra” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"The tradition is closely connected to Puri’s Jagannath religious culture and the hereditary practice of chitrakar families.","characteristics":"Firm line, decorative borders, tightly arranged figures, and vivid, mostly flat color.","themes":"Jagannath and Krishna narratives, episodes from epics, and regional devotional stories.","materials_technique":"Traditional cloth supports are layered and smoothed with a chalk-and-tamarind-gum preparation; mineral and other pigments and fine brushes are used.","cultural_significance":"Images support devotional life and preserve specialist painter knowledge while also circulating as portable works.","sources":["https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Orissa_Pattachitra/Orissa_Pattachitrawebpage.html","https://handicrafts.nic.in/pdf/LIST_OF_CRAFT_REGISTRED_UNDER_GEOGRAPHICAL_INDICATION_HANDICRAFTS.pdf"],"manual_review":false},"Patua":{"region":"West Bengal and adjoining regions; related scroll practices cross state borders.","description":"Patua painting refers broadly to scroll and picture-making by patua artists in Bengal. Painted panels can accompany songs and spoken narration, with images unfolding sequentially as a scroll is opened. Stories range from religious narratives to local events and social subjects. Materials, language, and performance practice vary between communities. This broad folder name is not the same as Kalighat painting, a separate urban style that also has links to patua painters. In this archive, the images are grouped under the exact “West Bengal → Patua” folder path. That placement follows the supplied collection structure; it does not mean the tradition is exclusive to that State/UT. The cited sources provide wider regional context where available. Because a folder contains only a selection of works, it cannot represent every maker, local variation, or historical phase.","history":"A living scroll tradition with regional histories in Bengal and neighboring areas.","characteristics":"Sequential painted scenes, strong outlines, and images composed to support narration.","themes":"Mythology, local stories, social issues, and contemporary events.","materials_technique":"Paper scrolls or cloth supports, pigments, and brushwork; practices differ by artist.","cultural_significance":"Painting and song together sustain a public storytelling practice.","sources":["https://www.nationalcraftsmuseum.nic.in/about-the-museum","https://handicrafts.nic.in/crafts/All_Crafts/Craft_Categories/Miscellaneous/Folk_Painting/Folk_Painting.html"],"manual_review":false}}};
